{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorusDivision","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorusDivision","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.SkewMonoidAlgebra.Support"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.exists_skew_right_division","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.exists_skew_right_division","kind":"theorem","line":219,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L219-L237","doc":"**Skew-Euclidean right-division (PROVED).**  In the localized torus\n(leading coefficient of `g` a unit) every `f` admits `f = q·g + r` with\nremainder `0` or of strictly smaller L-degree.  Proof: pick a fuel bound\nfrom the finite top degree of `f` (`topDeg_eq_bot_iff` gives an exponent\nwhen `f ≠ 0`) and run `exists_skew_right_division_fuel`.\n-- Ref: [leitner2026] NT-T2 brick B3, paper §1.2 (Ã_K principal);\n--      docs/workplans/2026-07-20-nguyen-tran-strong-aj-lean-workplan.md ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.exists_skew_right_division_fuel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.exists_skew_right_division_fuel","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L178-L217","doc":"**Fuel form of skew right-division.**  If `topDeg f < d + k` (the divisor\nhas top degree `d`), then `f = q·g + r` with `r = 0` or `topDeg r < topDeg g`.\nProof by induction on the fuel `k : ℕ`: each reduction step\n(`skew_right_division_degree_drop`) lowers `topDeg f` by at least one, so\n`k` steps suffice.  This is the concrete termination the (missing) Ore\nEuclidean algorithm would provide. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.topDeg_eq_bot_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.topDeg_eq_bot_iff","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L174-L176","doc":"`topDeg f = ⊥` exactly for the zero element. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.skew_right_division_degree_drop","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.skew_right_division_degree_drop","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L141-L172","doc":"**Full degree drop after one step (PROVED).**  When `topDeg g = d`,\n`topDeg f = e`, `d ≤ e` and the leading coefficient of `g` is a unit, one\nreduction step strictly lowers the top L-degree.  Proof: the `L^e`\ncoefficient vanishes (`reduction_top_coeff_cancel`) and every higher\ncoefficient vanishes because `f`'s top degree is `e` and the reduction\nmonomial `L^{e-d}·g` has top degree `≤ e` (computed directly via\n`coeff_single_mul`), so `topDeg_lt_of_forall_ge_eq_zero` applies.\n-- Ref: [leitner2026] NT-T2 brick B3;\n--      docs/workplans/2026-07-20-nguyen-tran-strong-aj-lean-workplan.md ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.topDeg_lt_of_forall_ge_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.topDeg_lt_of_forall_ge_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L125-L139","doc":"**`topDeg` upper bound from vanishing high coefficients.**  If every\nL-coefficient of `f` at exponent `≥ e` vanishes, then `topDeg f < e`.  This\nis the `WithBot ℤ`/`Finset.max` calculus that upgrades a coefficient-level\ncancellation to a strict top-degree drop. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.reduction_top_coeff_cancel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.reduction_top_coeff_cancel","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L116-L123","doc":"**Top-coefficient cancellation (PROVED).**  One reduction step removes the\n`L^e` term: the `L^e` coefficient of `f - reductionQuotient·g` is `0`.\nThis is the concrete degree-drop content — the step provably eliminates\nthe leading term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.coeffAt_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.coeffAt_sub","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L105-L114","doc":"Coefficients are additive under subtraction (`coeff_add` + negation). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.reduction_leading_coeff_match","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.reduction_leading_coeff_match","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L87-L103","doc":"**Leading-coefficient match (PROVED — the reduction engine).**  With\n`u = coeffAt g d` a unit, the `L^e` coefficient of\n`reductionQuotient f d e u · g` equals `coeffAt f e`.  This is the exact\ncancellation identity the skew division is built on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.reductionQuotient","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.reductionQuotient","kind":"def","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L79-L85","doc":"**The reduction quotient monomial.**  To cancel the `L^e` term of `f`\nagainst a divisor `g` of top L-degree `d` with leading coefficient `u`,\nmultiply `g` on the left by\n`L^{e-d} · (coeffAt f e · σ^{e-d}(u)⁻¹)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.isUnit_smul_of_isUnit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.QuantumTorus.isUnit_smul_of_isUnit","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/QuantumTorusDivision.lean#L73-L77","doc":"`σᵏ` preserves units: if `u` is a unit then so is `(ofAdd k) • u`\n(the action is by ring automorphisms). ","signatureChunk":0}]}